{"id": "077e9b5e5b803672:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:6fdc0a34ad75c037", "source_id": "077e9b5e5b803672", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/additional-resources/retirement-toolkit/employer-sponsored-plans/traditional-and-roth-401k-plans", "topic": "401(k) Plans", "subtopic": "401(k) Plans", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "With a traditional 401(k), the tax benefit is upfront. With a Roth 401(k), the tax benefit comes later.", "question": null, "answer": "A 401(k) plan is an employer-sponsored retirement plan with investment options. Traditional and Roth 401(k) accounts differ in when they provide tax benefits: traditional contributions and investment earnings are tax-deferred, so taxes are paid when savings are withdrawn. Roth contributions are made with after-tax dollars, while account earnings and withdrawals are generally tax-free. A plan may let you allocate money between both options.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1179, "output_tokens": 198}}
{"id": "077e9b5e5b803672:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:6fdc0a34ad75c037", "source_id": "077e9b5e5b803672", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/additional-resources/retirement-toolkit/employer-sponsored-plans/traditional-and-roth-401k-plans", "topic": "401(k) Plans", "subtopic": "401(k) Plans", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "In a traditional 401(k) account, employee contributions to a 401(k) plan and any earnings from the investments are tax-deferred. You pay the taxes on contributions and earnings when the savings are withdrawn. In a Roth 401(k) account, employee contributions are not tax-deferred but are made with after-tax dollars. Income earned on the account, from interest, dividends, or capital gains, and withdrawals are generally tax-free.", "question": "How does the tax timing differ between traditional and Roth 401(k) accounts?", "answer": "With a traditional 401(k), contributions and investment earnings are tax-deferred, and taxes are paid when the money is withdrawn. With a Roth 401(k), contributions are made with after-tax dollars, while account earnings and withdrawals are generally tax-free.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": false, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1179, "output_tokens": 355}}
